<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:vm="using:EmberTern.LicenseManager.ViewModels"
        xmlns:lm="using:EmberTern.LicenseManager"
        x:Class="EmberTern.LicenseManager.Views.SettingsWindow"
        x:DataType="vm:SettingsViewModel"
        Title="{lm:Loc Settings.WindowTitle}"
        Width="760" Height="620"
        MinWidth="640" MinHeight="420"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        Background="{DynamicResource BackgroundBrush}"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource ForegroundBrush}">

  <!-- ⭐⭐ DELIVERY IS ITS OWN RESPONSIBILITY, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS ITS OWN WINDOW (decision D‑5).

       Storage answers "how do I look after the register of record" — backup, restore, the data folder.
       This answers "how does a licence reach the customer". Folding them together would have meant
       editing a closed stage's window and rewriting a title chosen on purpose, to gain nothing.

       ⭐⭐ ONE STABLE SIZE, AND IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE PAGE (user QA, L6.1a).

       This window first shipped with `SizeToContent="Height"` and `CanResize="False"`, which measured
       WHICHEVER PAGE WAS SHOWING: General opened small, E-mail opened much taller, and switching between
       them resized the window under the operator. On the reporting screen the E-mail page did not fit at
       all. ⛔ That is not a spacing problem and it is NOT fixed by making the content smaller.

       ⭐ The shape is EmberTern's own `SettingsWindow`, taken rather than invented: a declared
       `Width`/`Height` with `MinWidth`/`MinHeight` floors, NO `SizeToContent`, rows `Auto,*,Auto` so the
       body FILLS, and a `ScrollViewer` around the content column ONLY. So General and E-mail occupy
       exactly the same rectangle, General simply has empty space below its one group — and stability beats
       minimising height, which is the trade the user named explicitly.

       ⭐ What must stay still, and why it does by CONSTRUCTION rather than by arrangement: the navigation
       and the hairline are sibling COLUMNS of the body grid, and the heading and footer are sibling ROWS
       of the outer grid. None of them is inside `PageScroll`, so none of them can scroll.

       ⚠ `SettingsWindow.axaml.cs` clamps the window to the screen's working area on open. It only ever
       SHRINKS — it is not a fit-to-content mechanism, and the height still never depends on the page.

       ⛔ No StackPanel `Spacing` in the body: every gap is owned by the element above it, through a
       role, so a caption never sits as far from its own field as two fields sit from each other. -->
  <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">

    <!-- Header ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <Border Grid.Row="0" Classes="dialog-header">
      <TextBlock Classes="h1" VerticalAlignment="Center" Name="PageHeading"
                 Text="{Binding SelectedPage.Title}" />
    </Border>

    <!-- Body ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <!-- ⭐⭐ THE SHAPE IS EMBERTERN'S SETTINGS CENTRE: a navigation column, a hairline, and ONE PAGE PER
         CATEGORY gated by IsVisible. ⛔ Deliberately not a generic page host — that would be an
         abstraction for pages that do not exist, and with two categories this is one block each with
         every binding still compiled and typed. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="180,Auto,*">

      <!-- ⚠ The navigation uses this application's OWN base ListBox styling rather than a copy of
           EmberTern's `settings-nav` (its 2 px selection indicator). Decision D‑7 narrowed the copying to
           the MENU block alone, and a second copied block would be a second thing to guard. The rows read
           correctly: selection and hover already come from the shared palette. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="0"
              Background="{DynamicResource PanelBrush}"
              Padding="{DynamicResource Pad.Panel}">
        <ListBox Name="PageList"
                 ItemsSource="{Binding Pages}"
                 SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedPage}"
                 Background="Transparent"
                 BorderThickness="0">
          <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SettingsPageViewModel">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="{DynamicResource Space.Md}">
                <!-- ⚠ The icon arrives as a KEY and is resolved by the same converter the message strip
                     uses — one mechanism, not a second one. -->
                <Viewbox Classes="icon" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <Canvas Width="24" Height="24">
                    <Path Data="{Binding IconKey,
                                 Converter={x:Static lm:IconGeometryConverter.Instance}}" />
                  </Canvas>
                </Viewbox>
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Title}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
        </ListBox>
      </Border>

      <Border Grid.Column="1"
              Width="{DynamicResource Stroke.Hairline}"
              Background="{DynamicResource BorderBrush}" />

      <!-- The Border stays as the pages' ancestor DELIBERATELY: `Border.dialog-body TextBox` is a
           DESCENDANT selector and is what gives every field its `Margin.FieldGap`. Replacing it with a
           plain Panel would have silently removed the vertical rhythm from every input on the E-mail
           page - a layout change with no error and no failing binding. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="2" Classes="dialog-body">
        <ScrollViewer Name="PageScroll"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
        <Panel>

        <!-- ══ GENERAL ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <StackPanel Name="GeneralPage" VerticalAlignment="Top" IsVisible="{Binding IsGeneralPage}">

          <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.Language}" />

          <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.ApplicationLanguage}" />
          <!-- ⭐⭐ DISABLED ON PURPOSE (decision D‑8). The row is here so the structure is the structure it
               will keep and the later localization stage has a place to land — but it stores NOTHING.
               A preference the operator can set and that then changes nothing is the exact defect that
               removed `ClientLibraryPath` from EmberTern's connection dialog. ⚠ The view model gives this
               picker no setter at all, so there is not even an accidental path to persisting it. -->
          <ComboBox Name="ApplicationLanguagePicker" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                    IsEnabled="False"
                    Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                    ItemsSource="{Binding ApplicationLanguageOptions}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding ApplicationLanguage, Mode=OneWay}"
                    ToolTip.Tip="{Binding ApplicationLanguageUnavailable}">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:LanguageOption">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption.Value}" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>

          <TextBlock Name="ApplicationLanguageNote" Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     Text="{Binding ApplicationLanguageUnavailable}" />

        </StackPanel>

        <!-- ══ E-MAIL ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <StackPanel Name="EmailPage" VerticalAlignment="Top" IsVisible="{Binding IsEmailPage}">

        <!-- ⭐ The first thing on screen is what the operator actually wants to know: what delivery is
             possible with what is typed right now. It follows the FIELDS, not the saved file.

             ⚠ `Border.card`, NOT `Border.message`: this is a standing description of state, not
             something that happened. The message strip below is the one surface that reports outcomes,
             and giving this one the same appearance would put two things that look identical on screen
             saying two different kinds of thing. -->
        <Border Classes="card" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}">
          <TextBlock Name="DeliverySummaryText" TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding DeliverySummary}" />
        </Border>

        <!-- ══ SENDER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <!-- ⚠ Above the server on purpose: a sender address alone is enough to compose and save an
             .eml, so it is the one field that is never optional. -->
        <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.Sender}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.FromAddressWhatCustomerReplies}" />
        <TextBox Name="FromAddressBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 PlaceholderText="{lm:Loc Settings.LicencjeTwojafirmaPl}"
                 Text="{Binding FromAddress}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.FromNameOptional}" />
        <TextBox Name="FromNameBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                 PlaceholderText="EmberTern"
                 Text="{Binding FromName}" />

        <!-- ══ SERVER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <!-- ⭐ Every one of these is DATA. The L6 measurement was taken on a test mailbox and the
             production one has not been measured yet, so moving to it must be four fields typed here
             rather than a code change. ⛔ No provider name appears anywhere in this application. -->
        <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.ServerLeaveEmptyDeliverByFile}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.SmtpHost}" />
        <TextBox Name="HostBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 Text="{Binding Host}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.Port}" />
        <TextBox Name="PortBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 Text="{Binding PortText}" />

        <!-- ⚠ Two choices, not three: System.Net.Mail.SmtpClient implements STARTTLS only, so implicit
             TLS (port 465) is deliberately absent rather than offered and inert — the defect that
             removed ClientLibraryPath from EmberTern's connection dialog. See SmtpSettings. -->
        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.TransportSecurity}" />
        <ComboBox Name="SecurityPicker" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                  Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding SecurityOptions}"
                  SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedSecurity}">
          <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SmtpSecurityOption">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption.Value}" />
            </DataTemplate>
          </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
        </ComboBox>

        <!-- ══ CREDENTIALS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.SignIn}" />

        <!-- ⚠ Separate from the sender address on purpose: a relay commonly authenticates as one
             identity and sends as another, and collapsing the two makes that unreachable. -->
        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.UsernameAccountSigns}" />
        <TextBox Name="UsernameBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 Text="{Binding Username}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label"
                   Text="{lm:Loc Settings.PasswordGmailMailboxAppPasswordNot}" />
        <TextBox Name="PasswordBox" PasswordChar="•"
                 Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 Text="{Binding Password}" />

        <!-- ⭐ DPAPI's non-portability is invisible until it bites, so it is said here rather than
             discovered after a move to a new machine — the same warning EmberTern gives for
             connection passwords. -->
        <TextBlock Name="ProtectionNoteText" Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                   Text="{Binding ProtectionNote}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.StoredIn}" />
        <SelectableTextBlock Name="SettingsPathText" Classes="mono" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                             Text="{Binding SettingsPath}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.TestConfiguration}" />

        <!-- L6.3. The address is TYPED, and there is deliberately no picker that could offer a customer's:
             a diagnostic that can be aimed at a customer by a mis-click eventually will be, and they would
             receive an unexplained message from a licensing system. It is not stored either - it is a
             scratch value for one attempt, not a setting. -->
        <TextBlock Classes="field-label"
                   Text="{lm:Loc Settings.SendTestMessageYourOwnAddress}" />
        <TextBox Name="TestRecipientBox" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                 PlaceholderText="{lm:Loc Settings.YouExampleCom}"
                 Text="{Binding TestRecipient}" />

        <!-- The test uses what is ON THIS PAGE, saved or not: an operator who has just typed a host
             expects the test to try THAT host, and requiring a Save first would mean persisting a
             configuration in order to discover it is wrong. -->
        <TextBlock Name="TestNoteText" Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                   Text="{lm:Loc Settings.ItSendsRealMessageThroughSettings}" />

        <Button Classes="flat" Name="SendTestEmail" Content="{lm:Loc Settings.SendTestEmail}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                ToolTip.Tip="{lm:Loc Settings.SendsTestMessageAddressAboveAfter}"
                Command="{Binding SendTestEmailCommand}" />

        <!-- ══ MESSAGE LANGUAGE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <TextBlock Classes="section" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.MessageLanguage}" />

        <TextBlock Classes="field-label" Text="{lm:Loc Settings.TheLanguageLicenceEMailWritten}" />
        <ComboBox Name="MessageLanguagePicker" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                  Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.FieldGap}"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding MessageLanguageOptions}"
                  SelectedItem="{Binding MessageLanguage}">
          <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:LanguageOption">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption.Value}" />
            </DataTemplate>
          </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
        </ComboBox>

        <!-- ⚠ Both independences said out loud, because the pairing is the surprising part: the interface
             language and the message language are two values, and this one applies to EVERY customer
             (decision D‑4 declined a per-customer language column). -->
        <TextBlock Name="MessageLanguageNote" Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{DynamicResource Margin.SectionGap}"
                   Text="{lm:Loc Settings.ItIndependentInterfaceLanguageAppliesEvery}" />

        <!-- ⭐ ONE message surface, and the LAST child: the body band's padding separates it from the
             footer, so it carries no bottom margin of its own.

             ⚠⚠ This is the FULL recipe — severity stripe, severity glyph, one `Pad.Cell` inset owned by
             one container — the same markup `MainWindow` carries. It is spelled out rather than reduced
             to a bare TextBlock because `Border.message` sets `Padding` to 0 SO THAT the stripe can
             reach three edges: without the inner grid the text sits flush against the border and the
             severity is carried by a hairline that is nearly invisible in Light. -->
        <Border Classes="message" Classes.info="{Binding IsInfo}" Classes.success="{Binding IsSuccess}"
                Classes.warning="{Binding IsWarning}" Classes.error="{Binding IsError}"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="3,*">
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Classes="severity-stripe" />
            <Grid Grid.Column="1" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="{DynamicResource Space.Sm}"
                  Margin="{DynamicResource Pad.Cell}">
              <Viewbox Grid.Column="0" Classes="icon" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Canvas Width="24" Height="24">
                  <Path Data="{Binding MessageIconKey,
                               Converter={x:Static lm:IconGeometryConverter.Instance}}" />
                </Canvas>
              </Viewbox>
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Name="EmailSettingsMessageText" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding MessageText}" />
            </Grid>
          </Grid>
        </Border>

        </StackPanel>

        </Panel>
        </ScrollViewer>
      </Border>

    </Grid>

    <!-- Footer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <!-- ⭐ ONE primary action, and it BELONGS TO THE E-MAIL PAGE — the rule StorageWindow established:
         an action is never on screen beside a page it cannot act on. General carries no action at all
         today, because its one row is a disabled placeholder that stores nothing (D‑8).
         ⚠ Save covers the whole E-mail page, message language included; see SettingsViewModel.
         Revert and Forget are `flat` because neither is what the operator opened this window to do, and
         Forget is destructive in the smallest possible way — it deletes fields that can be retyped,
         never anything signed. -->
    <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="dialog-footer">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="{DynamicResource Space.Md}"
                  IsVisible="{Binding IsEmailPage}">
        <Button Classes="flat" Name="Forget" Content="{lm:Loc Settings.ForgetSettings}"
                ToolTip.Tip="{lm:Loc Settings.DeletesStoredEMailConfigurationIncluding}"
                Command="{Binding ForgetCommand}" />
        <Button Classes="flat" Name="Revert" Content="{lm:Loc Settings.RevertAction}"
                ToolTip.Tip="{lm:Loc Settings.DiscardsEditsReloadsWhatSaved}"
                Command="{Binding RevertCommand}" />
        <Button Classes="primary" Name="SaveSettings" Content="{lm:Loc Settings.SaveAction}"
                IsDefault="True"
                Command="{Binding SaveCommand}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

  </Grid>
</Window>
